Apache Iceberg version
main (development). Reproduced at db43b88; the same code paths are in 1.12.0 (latest release).
Query engine
None (core API).
Please describe the bug 🐞
RewriteManifests.addManifest does not check the format version of the manifest it is given. On a v2+ table, a manifest written in v1 format (no sequence_number / file_sequence_number columns) is mishandled on both paths.
Reused manifest (snapshotId() is null, snapshot ID inheritance enabled): the commit succeeds and the v1 manifest is added to the manifest list as-is, with the new commit's sequence number. On read, BaseInheritableMetadata only fills in sequence numbers for ADDED entries or when the manifest's sequence number is 0, so the EXISTING entries come back with null data and file sequence numbers. Scan planning then throws a NullPointerException, because DeleteFileIndex.forEntry passes entry.dataSequenceNumber() to forDataFile(long, DataFile).
Copied manifest (snapshotId() is -1): ManifestFiles.copyRewriteManifest reads the entries with empty inheritable metadata, so the sequence numbers stay null and the v2+ writer fails inside addManifest with:
IllegalStateException: Found unassigned sequence number for an entry from snapshot: <id>

The spec says that "when reading v1 manifests with no sequence number column, sequence numbers for all files must default to 0", and that existing entries written to a new manifest must carry non-null sequence numbers.
Reproduction
In TestRewriteManifests with format version 2 or later:
table.newFastAppend().appendFile(FILE_A).appendFile(FILE_B).commit();
Snapshot first = table.currentSnapshot();
ManifestFile original = Iterables.getOnlyElement(first.allManifests(table.io()));

OutputFile out = table.ops().io().newOutputFile(temp.resolve("v1-manifest.avro").toString());
ManifestWriter<DataFile> writer = ManifestFiles.write(1, table.spec(), out, null);
try {
  writer.existing(FILE_A, first.snapshotId(), first.sequenceNumber(), first.sequenceNumber());
  writer.existing(FILE_B, first.snapshotId(), first.sequenceNumber(), first.sequenceNumber());
} finally {
  writer.close();
}

table.rewriteManifests().deleteManifest(original).addManifest(writer.toManifestFile()).commit();

// the committed entries read back with null data and file sequence numbers
Lists.newArrayList(table.newScan().planFiles()); // NullPointerException
Writing the manifest with snapshot ID -1L instead of null takes the copy path, and addManifest throws the IllegalStateException above. Both behaviors reproduce on format versions 2, 3 and 4.
Expected behavior
addManifest should handle v1-format manifests on v2+ tables consistently: either reject them with a clear error, or copy them and write the spec default of 0 as explicit sequence numbers. It should not commit a manifest that breaks scan planning.
This came up while reviewing the same operation in iceberg-cpp (apache/iceberg-cpp#774), which copies v1-format manifests and writes sequence number 0.

Apache Iceberg version
main (development).
Query engine
Other — Java API reproduction.
Please describe the bug 🐞
Scan planning is slow with large numbers of files, even with fast manifest reads:

HadoopCatalog + HadoopFileIO on local disk.
S3 + a caching FileIO, with manifest cache hits.

When the result queue is empty and tasks are pending, ParallelIterable.hasNext() sleeps for 10 ms. Results arriving or tasks completing during that sleep do not wake the consumer. Repeated polling delays can dominate fast reads.
In a synthetic test with 1,000 one-element tasks, 10 threads, 20 task slots, and a 1 ms delayed start per task, the original took about 505 ms (50 sleeps) versus 54 ms with notification-based waiting, approximately 9.4× faster (medians of 10 runs after 2 warm-ups; not an end-to-end storage benchmark).
Could we wake the consumer on data arrival, completion, failure, or closure instead of polling?

Minimal reproduction against the original implementation (imports omitted)
public class ParallelIterablePollingRepro {
  public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
    System.setProperty("iceberg.worker.num-threads", "10");
    ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor workers = new ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor(10) {
      @Override
      public void execute(Runnable command) {
        schedule(command, 1, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
      }
    };
    workers.prestartAllCoreThreads();

    try {
      List<Iterable<Integer>> inputs = Collections.nCopies(1000, List.of(1));
      long start = System.nanoTime();
      int count = 0;
      try (ParallelIterable<Integer> results = new ParallelIterable<>(inputs, workers)) {
        for (Integer ignored : results) {
          count++;
        }
      }
      System.out.printf("items=%d elapsed=%.3f ms%n", count,
          (System.nanoTime() - start) / 1_000_000.0);
    } finally {
      workers.shutdownNow();
      workers.awaitTermination(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    }
  }
}

